From f4ea318b0b71718e83c19b0e0efd208379bfc91c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JuggyMcNutty <11588877+JuggyMcNutty@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 08:57:55 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] VM: one evaluator per statement, nested values returned
 directly

FORK ONLY. This project ships a NO-AI Code Rule asking that LLM-written
changes stay in a fork and are never PR'd upstream. These were written with
Claude. Do not submit them.

Every expression node -- every constant, variable read and operator
argument -- was evaluated by a new ExpressionEvaluator holding a whole
ExpressionEvalResult (a value, a label, an iterator, the control-flow
fields), checked against the breakpoint list, and moved out through two
more copies of its value. Profiling Liberty Island's game tick on the
desktop (perf with frame pointers, samples under ULevel::Tick) put the
interpreter's own overhead at over half of the tick.

- A statement gets one evaluator and one result. Nested expressions are
  evaluated by that same evaluator into a value the caller owns
  (ExpressionEvaluator::Value), which comes back without copies.
- Only the statement says what the frame does next. A nested expression's
  control flow (a None context's AccessedNone, say) is dropped exactly as
  it was when it had a result of its own; Skip, Context and ClassContext
  still pass their inner statement's through.
- Breakpoints are only ever set on statements, so only statements are
  checked against them. Frame::StepExpression is kept per node as before.
- ExpressionValue gains a move assignment: every value is handed up by
  assignment, and a copy would duplicate strings and structs.
- A jump found its target statement in a std::map of every expression in
  the function. Statements are read in offset order, so a binary search
  over their offsets gives the same index; a temporary check compared the
  two for every expression offset and 1 in 97 of all others in every
  function loaded on Liberty Island: 6.2 million lookups, all equal.
- NameToBool compared against the spelling "None", a hash lookup per
  test; None is name index 0.

On the desktop, script time on Liberty Island went from ~2.3 to ~1.95 ms a
frame. Its log is the same as before.
